function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:`${e.marker}) ${r}`}})}var t={id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp`,title:`A Club Where Lions Dance and Traditions Take Root`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`The first thing you notice in the staircase to the fourth-floor studio on Canal Street in Manhattan, New York, is the measured thumping (嘭嘭声) coming from behind a metal door. Just beyond the entrance, large lion masks were twisting and turning to the drum-beat. On a recent Friday evening, the teenagers made their way across the studio floor as they practiced Chinese lion dancing.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`"You want to play in a circle," Victor Fong, aged 24, told his students at the New York Chinese Freemasons Athletic Club. "Take it slow and do it again." The dance group, made up of 60 members, performs throughout the year but was now preparing for its biggest events, Chinese Lunar New Year celebrations, which will begin on Saturday and conclude on February 15. Teenagers comprise about half of the group, many of whom began lion dancing at the age of 14. About 100 current and past members of the dance group—which has been performing since 1956—will be among the 220 groups marching through Chinatown in Manhattan for the 18th Lunar New Year Parade on February 5.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Mr. Fong has been involved in the organization since he was 15 and began teaching lion dancing three years ago. But the club also acts as a recreation center and safe haven (庇护所) for teenagers, with video games readily available. As many as 20 students show up after school. "The basic requirement for hanging out here is you have to learn how to lion dance," Mr. Fong said. While that is not a formal requirement for being a member, everyone finds a way to participate in the Lunar New Year Parade, whether it's by dancing, carrying a flag or beating a drum.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`Alvin Chau, aged 26, is an environmental consultant by day and a lion dancer on weekends. He has been a club member for 10 years and joined because of an interest in lion dance. "We're a big family," he said, shaking hands with other members as they walked through the door. "You know everyone."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`It is believed that the lion dance began in the third century. Stories vary about how lion dancing came to be, but most of them include a monster named Nian who would terrorize a village. The villagers finally banded together and scared the beast away with firecrackers (爆竹) and drums. While lions are not native to China, some versions of the story include the villagers creating a monster of their own in the shape of a lion to fight off the beast.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`Today, the dancers travel across Chinatown going from business to business to bring good luck for the coming year. The new year-according to the Chinese lunar calendar-will begin on Saturday and marks the Year of the Rooster (鸡年), which is thought to be a symbol of positivity. "It's almost like the dark night is ending and the sun is coming up," said Ya Yung Teng, the digital collections coordinator for the Museum of Chinese in America. "It's hopeful that we're going to have a new day." Roosters and chickens are not particularly strong creatures, Ms. Teng said, but they are numerous. "In a way," she said, the rooster "stands for 'We the People.'"`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`The lion head and tail are operated by two people. As one person controls the head, a second follows under a train of fabric representing the body. The dancers move in harmony as they imitate the animal's natural movements in the wild, including the aggressiveness of an attack. A lion head figure weighs under 10 pounds and sits squarely on the dancer's shoulders. Inside, the dancer manipulates strings that open and close the lion's eyes, shake its ears and open its mouth to reveal a fire-orange tongue.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`"A good lion dancer will imitate a living creature," said Karlin Chan, aged 59, who heads the athletic club's community public relations. "I started lion dancing when I was a kid," he said. "Chinatown was much smaller then and it was a celebration with fireworks and firecrackers, which added a lot of flavor and meaning to it."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Mr. Chan buys a new lion head each year when he travels to China. A head costs about $1,500. "If you want the good stuff, the quality, you have to see it for yourself," he said. "I'll inspect the product before we put it in the container and ship it over." Mr. Chan, who has been involved with the club for nearly 50 years, said that passing the dance from one generation to the next was vital. "You need to pass on the traditions and the culture, and this is a part of our culture," he said. "It's a great way to promote cultural understanding and exchange; we welcome that."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`For Sara Pore, aged 17, another club member, lion dance is more than just tradition, it provides a creative outlet. "Lion dancing started 2,000 years ago—that's incredible," she said. "But what makes you a competent lion dancer is that there's a sense of imagination involved. Lion dancing teaches competence in leadership because of this. You're constantly forced to push yourself past your limit."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Back at rehearsal (排练), Justin Le, aged 18, tied a red ribbon around his waist to practice jumping. The dancers use the ribbons to pull themselves up over their partners' heads. The room's ceilings are too low to wear the lion heads for jumping practice, so once up on his partner's head, Mr. Le held out his arms as if in offering.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Mr. Le comes by the dance as a legacy (传承). "I was born into it," Mr. Le said, noting that his uncle and father were club members. "Growing up, I would always watch my family and see the lion dance, and I slowly grew interested in it." By the age of 14, he was fully enrolled in the athletic club's lion dance group. "I value my culture and tradition, being Chinese or Asian-American. I have a lot of pride in that," he said. "I want to contribute and give back to the community."`}]},n={"cet4-2023-06-s3":{subtitle:`2023 年 6 月 · 第 3 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2023年6月英语四级第3套真题，写作考大学生社区服务的益处与可提供的服务，翻译考中国义务教育，听力与第1套完全相同并给出原文由站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: Suppose the student union of your university is organizing an online discussion on college students doing community service. You are to write an essay on its benefits and the service you can provide to the community. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`Community service is of great significance for college students. Firstly, it cultivates a sense of social responsibility among students. By participating in various community activities, students can better understand the needs of society and contribute to its development. Moreover, community service enhances students' practical skills and teamwork abilities. It provides a platform for students to apply their knowledge and skills in real-world situations, fostering a deeper understanding of their field of study. Personally, I can tutor children on weekends and help organize neighborhood clean-ups. Other students can also offer diverse services. Students majoring in education can tutor children, while those studying environmental science can organize environmental protection campaigns. Medical students can offer health check-ups and basic medical advice, and students from various other disciplines can contribute their expertise to improve community welfare. In conclusion, engaging in community service not only benefits the community but also enriches the personal growth of students. It is a win-win situation that should be encouraged and promoted.`,guidance:[`第1段亮明观点与益处：Community service is of great significance for college students 开篇点题，Firstly / Moreover 分层展开——培养社会责任感、理解社会需求、提升实践能力与团队合作，正面回应 Directions 中的 benefits 要求。`,`第2段写"我能提供的服务"：以 Personally 引出个人做法（周末辅导孩子、组织社区清洁），再用 Other students can also offer diverse services 扩展到不同专业——教育专业辅导孩子、环境科学专业组织环保活动、医科学生提供体检与基础医疗建议，服务具体、覆盖面广，紧扣 Directions 中的 the service you can provide。`,`第3段总结升华：In conclusion 后以 not only… but also… 概括社区服务"惠及社区、成长自我"的双赢，末句 It is a win-win situation that should be encouraged and promoted 呼应主题、表明倡议立场。`,`语言与字数：全文用 Firstly / Moreover / Personally / In conclusion 衔接，层次清晰；词数控制在 120–180 词之间，符合题目要求。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`Suppose the student union of your university is organizing an online discussion on college students doing community service. You are to write an essay on its benefits and the service you can provide to the community. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`命题作文，两个任务点缺一不可：大学生做社区服务的益处、自己能提供的服务。参考范文第1段用 Firstly / Moreover 从社会责任感、实践能力与团队合作写益处；第2段以 Personally 引出个人做法（周末辅导孩子、组织社区清洁），再按专业列举他人可提供的多样服务；末段 In conclusion 用 not only… but also… 总结"双赢"并倡议推广。三段结构，词数 120–180，可据自身情况替换具体服务细节。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear three news reports. At the end of each news report, you will hear two or three questions. Both the news report and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear three passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`A woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding wild animals. The 55-year-old woman's neighbours blame her for making the area's rat problem worse. Newly installed cameras captured several rats active in the middle of the day. Neighbours say that it's even worse during the night. The woman and her parents, who own the home, told reporters that she's being charged for feeding birds. "Who would have known just loving animals gets you that much trouble," she said. It is prohibited to feed any wild animals, including birds, in Rhode Island City. Nevertheless, while the woman's intention was to feed birds, it is clear that rats were also benefiting. Antony Moretti, Director of the City Administration, said he saw more than 20 rats near the woman's home. He said it will take months to get the problem under control.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`To prepare for eventually sending astronauts to Mars, NASA began taking applications Friday for four people to live for a year in Mars Dune Alpha. That's a 1,700-square-foot Martian habitat inside a building in Houston. The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars. They will have limited communications with family, restricted food and resources. NASA is planning three experiments with the first one starting in the fall next year. Food will all be ready to eat space food. Some plants will be grown, but not potatoes like in the movie The Martian. "We want to understand how humans perform in them," said Lead Scientist Grace Douglas. "We're looking at Mars' realistic situations." The application process opened Friday and they're not seeking just anybody. The requirements are strict, including a master's degree in a science, engineering or math field or pilot experience. Only American citizens or permanent U.S. residents are acceptable. Applicants must be between 30 and 55 and in good physical health. Attitude is key, said former Canadian astronaut Chris Hadfield. He said the participants need to be super competent, resourceful and not rely on other people to feel comfortable.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`A Japanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player. Nagoya Mayor Takashi Kawamura had praised athlete Mio Goto during a public media event. He asked her to put the medal around his neck. Kawamura then bit into it. Biting a medal in front of journalists and photographers has become a common pose for Olympic medalists. However, it is only for the winners themselves, not others. "I'm really sorry that I hurt the treasure of the gold medalist," Kawamura told reporters Thursday. The mayor said the medal was undamaged, though he offered to pay for the cost of a new one. Goto, however, has accepted the International Olympic Committee's offer of a replacement, according to Japanese media reports. The scene broadcast on television prompted thousands of complaints to City Hall. Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures and that it was disrespectful and unacceptable for Kawamura to bite one. "I would cry if that happened to me," said another athlete, Naohisa Takato. "I handle my own gold medal so gently that I would not scratch it."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`M: What's that orange thing on your computer screen? W: It's something I bought yesterday. It's a plastic sheet that blocks blue light. I have one that I use to cover my phone screen too. M: What do you mean by blue light? W: Blue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural. For example, from computers, phones, televisions, and other electronic devices. M: So blue light is harmful and that's why you want to block it? W: It isn't that simple. Blue light isn't necessarily bad for us. In fact, we need blue light during the day to be healthy. But too much blue light, especially from electronic devices, can harm our health by weakening our vision and making it harder for us to fall asleep. And poor sleep can cause all sorts of health problems. M: I'm not so sure that sleep is nearly as important as people always say it is. I haven't slept enough in months because I have too much work to do. And I feel fine. And it's the same for most of my friends. Poor sleep might be a problem for older people, but surely young people can handle late nights. W: Well, the research I've read shows that sleep is probably even more important than we thought, and that not having enough sleep can contribute to serious health problems, like obesity and heart disease. And all the artificial blue light from electronic devices means we have to try harder to sleep well. M: Maybe you're right. I'm on my computer very late most nights, and that's probably why I don't sleep enough.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`M: As a kid, did you know what job you wanted to do when you grew up? W: No, I didn't. And I got sick every time adults asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up. M: It's the same with me, and I'm tired of people asking that question of my 10-year-old daughter. My daughter's stock answers are basketball player, pop singer, mechanical engineer. W: Adults love that last one, as it's the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious. When she was much younger, my daughter used to say she wanted to be queen of the clouds, which I loved. That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children, springing from their boundless imaginations. M: Yes, we grown-ups can be tedious and limiting in our need for reality, and we teach a very gloomy image of adulthood, that whatever our children's future holds, it must be seen within the context of a job. How utterly overwhelming and dull. When people ask my son what he wants to be when he grows up, I have to swallow the urge to say, hey, back off my kid's dreams. W: We can't dismiss the idea that teenagers have to plan to do something after they finish school, and parents are entitled to hope it's more than simply spending 10 hours a day playing computer games. M: But asking, what do you want to be? isn't going to lead a child to a fulfilled life, rather it leads to false expectations and a high chance of disappointment. W: Exactly. We should be helping our kids understand who they are, even if that means letting go of who we think they should be.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Greater internet access correlates directly with improved health care, education, and economic development. People living in rural areas, however, lag behind in online use, which limits their access to government services, banking, and job opportunities. Nowhere is this challenge clearer than in Africa. Most Africans live in rural areas that are tough to wire for internet access. Now, some phone companies are trying to introduce internet-ready phones into African markets. Certain companies have started selling simple smartphones for only $20. Previously, the lowest price had been around $40, well out of reach for many people. These devices are powered by software from the giant electronics company KaiOS Technologies Ltd. Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable, but KaiOS went the other way. It made every effort to keep the essential capabilities of smartphones, but strip out costs and preserve battery life for people who likely have inadequate access to electricity. The KaiOS devices offer an alternative to the more expensive models that remain out of reach of many Africans and contribute to the digital divide. The body of KaiOS phones is as basic as it gets. Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad. They're designed for 3G networks because 4G coverage doesn't reach two-thirds of Africa's customers. In total, KaiOS phones are made from about $15 worth of parts, while Apple's top-of-the-line iPhone has $390 worth of stuff.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`For years, using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap. By contrast, fossil fuel plastic was more expensive. Thus, the sustainable option was an economic option too. But now, it is cheaper for major manufacturers to use new plastic. According to one recent business report, recycled plastic now costs an extra $72 a ton compared with newly made plastic. This may be because of consumer demands. They are pushing for more recycled plastics in new products. Meanwhile, new plastic is becoming cheaper. This is because of a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S. The price increase of recycled plastic could cost sustainable manufacturers an extra $250 million a year. Smaller manufacturers may also be forced to use new plastic to reduce costs. Makers of clear plastic bottles may also opt for new fossil fuel-based plastic to save money. Plastic packaging makers are being pressured to use more recycled plastic. This is done in hopes of reducing the enormous amount of plastic pollution in the oceans. The U.K. government plans to tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in their products. Additionally, the government is planning to increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market. This could mean incentives for new recycling plants. Additionally, recycling facilities may be improved at a local council level and recycled plastic could be imported. This would help increase the amount of recycled plastic in circulation.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan, naps are for sale. A 45-minute session in a darkened enclosure with peace and quiet costs $25. To be clear, this institution is no hotel. This is a nap joint. It sells the idea of the nap as much as the nap itself. Is a nap worth $25? The answer is obviously yes. Here, at this point in the argument, it's traditional for me to bring up all the studies that show the benefits of napping. But do you really need experts to tell you that? Just look at the world around you at 2.30 in the afternoon. I've been working from home for more than 10 years now and the quality and quantity of work I can do emerges directly from my ability to concentrate. I don't understand how people have creative careers without napping. Every day at about 1 p.m., everyone faces the same choice. Sleep until 2 p.m. and then work until 5, or daydream and drift around social media and attend pointless meetings until 7 p.m. The friends I have who still work in offices inform me their bosses insist that they take the second option and that napping is associated with laziness. I genuinely find it odd, for if you nap properly, it's like waking up from a full night's sleep and you can double your day's worth of concentration.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-1`,stem:`What do we learn from the report about the 55-year-old woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She was involved in a conflict with bird lovers.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She was charged with mistreating animals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She was on bad terms with her neighbors.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She was accused of violating a city law.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "a woman was charged with allegedly violating a Rhode Island city law against feeding wild animals"，这名 55 岁女子被指控违反罗得岛城市禁止喂野生动物的法律，即被控违反城市法规，选 D；B) mistreating animals（虐待动物）与原文 feeding（喂食）不符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-1`,stem:`What did the Director of the City Administration say at the end of the news report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It will take time to solve the rat problem.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) All wild animals should be well protected.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The woman was not to blame for the situation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) No one should go unpunished for violating law.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "it will take months to get the problem under control"，市政主管说要把鼠患控制住需要几个月，即解决问题需要时间，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-2`,stem:`What does NASA require the paid volunteers to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Communicate with astronauts in Mars Dune Alpha.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Work in an environment resembling Mars.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Build a Martian habitat in Houston.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Send in applications before Friday.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "The paid volunteers will work in an environment similar to Mars"，受薪志愿者将在与火星相似的环境中工作，选 B；火星栖息地是休斯敦已有的建筑，并非让志愿者去建造，C 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-2`,stem:`What will the participants in the project eat?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ready-made food.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Food that is organic.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Food they grow.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Potatoes mostly.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Food will all be ready to eat space food"，食物全部是即食太空食品，选 A；虽会种一些植物，但明确 "not potatoes like in the movie The Martian"，C、D 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What does the news report say about the Japanese Mayor Takashi Kawamura?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He apologized for scratching an athlete's gold medal.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He was asked to present a gold medal to Miu Goto.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He bit a softball player's Olympic gold medal.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He attracted public attention at a media event.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "A Japanese mayor apologized Thursday for biting the Olympic gold medal of a softball player"，名古屋市长咬了垒球运动员的奥运金牌并为此道歉，选 C；A) scratching（刮花）与原文 the medal was undamaged 不符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What did Mayor Takashi Kawamura offer to do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Have another picture taken with the Olympic medalist.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Apologize to the International Olympic Committee.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Get the damaged medal repaired.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay for the cost of a new medal.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "he offered to pay for the cost of a new one"，市长主动提出支付一枚新金牌的费用，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`,stem:`What did some Olympians say they would do with their medals?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Allow no one to touch them.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) See them as symbols of honor.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Treat them as treasures.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Keep them in a safe place.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Some Olympians said they treat their medals as treasures"，一些奥运选手说把奖牌视为珍宝，选 C；末句 "I handle my own gold medal so gently that I would not scratch it" 进一步印证。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What did the woman do to her computer?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She covered its screen with a plastic sheet.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She connected it with her smartphone.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She decorated it with colorful stickers.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She bought some new software for it.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文女士说买了 "a plastic sheet that blocks blue light" 贴在电脑屏幕上，也有一张贴在手机屏幕上，即在屏幕上覆盖防蓝光塑料膜，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What does the woman say about blue light?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It may not be simply blue.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It includes unnatural light.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is more harmful to young people.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It induces people to fall asleep.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Blue light includes natural light, but it also includes light that isn't natural"，蓝光既含自然光，也含来自电脑、手机等非自然光源的光，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`,stem:`Why does the man say he hasn't slept enough for months?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He has had much trouble falling asleep.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He has had some sort of health problems.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He has stayed up playing computer games.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He has been burdened with excessive work.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "I haven't slept enough in months because I have too much work to do"，男士说几个月睡眠不足是因为工作太多、负担过重，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`,stem:`What has the woman learned from the research she has read?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Exposure to blue light is the chief cause of obesity.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sleep may be more important than people assumed.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Sleep may also be negatively affected by natural light.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Overuse of electronic devices may cause heart disease.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "sleep is probably even more important than we thought"，女士读到的研究表明睡眠可能比人们原先以为的更重要，选 B；肥胖、心脏病是睡眠不足可能导致的健康问题，蓝光并非其 chief cause，A、D 偷换因果。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What question were both speakers fed up with when they were kids?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) What they wanted to be when grown up.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) What their favorite cartoon character was.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) What they learned from computer games.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) What they liked to do most after school.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`对话开头女士说 "I got sick every time adults asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up"，男士回应 "It's the same with me"，两人都厌倦被问长大后想做什么，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What occupation do adults see as both sensible and ambitious, according to the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A stock broker.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) A pop singer.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A mechanical engineer.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A basketball player.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "My daughter's stock answers are basketball player, pop singer, mechanical engineer" 后女士说 "Adults love that last one, as it's the perfect mix of the sensible and the ambitious"，last one 指机械工程师，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What kind of goal setting does the woman like to see in children?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Ambitious.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Sensible.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Imaginative.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Practical.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "That's the kind of goal-setting I like to see in children, springing from their boundless imaginations"，女士赞许孩子 "想当云朵女王" 这类源自无尽想象的目标设定，即富有想象力，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman suggest adults should do?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Relax their strict control of their kids.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Help their kids understand themselves.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Impose their own dreams on their kids.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Dismiss their high expectations of their kids.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`对话末句 "We should be helping our kids understand who they are, even if that means letting go of who we think they should be"，女士建议帮助孩子认识自己、放下成人预设的期待，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What are a number of phone companies trying to do in Africa?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Set up company branches.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Improve its infrastructure.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Introduce iPhones into its markets.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Promote Internet-ready phones.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "some phone companies are trying to introduce internet-ready phones into African markets"，一些手机公司正试图把可上网手机引入非洲市场，选 D；文中提到 20 美元的简易智能机，并非苹果 iPhone，C 不对。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`,stem:`How do KaiOS smartphones differ from smartphones of most other companies?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They cater to Africans' needs.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are more expensive models.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are more powerful and capable.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They boast the longest battery life.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Most companies are trying to make phones ever more powerful and capable, but KaiOS went the other way… keep the essential capabilities… strip out costs and preserve battery life"，KaiOS 反其道而行，保留基本功能、削减成本、保住续航，正是迎合非洲用户的使用条件与需求，选 A；D) the longest battery life 属无根据的最高级。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`,stem:`What are KaiOS smartphones equipped with?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) A large touchscreen.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) An old-school keypad.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A voice-response device.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A digitally-designed system.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Instead of a touchscreen, they're controlled with an old-school keypad"，KaiOS 手机没有触摸屏，而用老式键盘操控，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`,stem:`What is said about using recycled plastic to make plastic products in the past?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It ensured sustainable economic growth.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It was strongly opposed by manufacturers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It was cheaper than using fossil fuel plastic.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It satisfied consumer demands on the whole.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "For years, using recycled plastic to make plastic products was cheap. By contrast, fossil fuel plastic was more expensive"，过去再生塑料比化石燃料塑料便宜，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`,stem:`What has led to a more competitive price for new plastic?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The capacity to mass produce it.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The U.S. federal government's regulations.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) A boom in market demand for clear plastic bottles.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) A rapid increase in U.S. petroleum chemical production.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "new plastic is becoming cheaper. This is because of a boom in petroleum chemical production from the U.S."，美国石化生产激增使新塑料价格更具竞争力，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`,stem:`What does the U.K. government plan to do about plastic?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Require companies to use 30% of new plastic.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Increase the supply of new plastic in the market.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Reduce the amount of plastic pollution in local areas.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Take measures to promote the use of recycled plastic.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文英国政府计划 "tax companies which don't use at least 30% recycled plastic in their products"，并 "increase the quantity of recycled plastic in the market"，即对不用足 30% 再生塑料的公司征税并增加再生塑料供应，整体是促进再生塑料的使用，选 D；A、B 把 recycled 错写成 new plastic。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What do we learn about the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It studies dreams.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It rents a place for nap-takers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is a hotel for business people.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It is a nap research institute.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "At the Dreamery, a business in Manhattan, naps are for sale. A 45-minute session in a darkened enclosure… costs $25"，这家店按 45 分钟出租昏暗安静的打盹空间，明确 "this institution is no hotel"，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`,stem:`Why does the speaker ask us to look at the world around us at 2.30 in the afternoon?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To find out creative people's work performance.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To see how many people can go without napping.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To understand the obvious importance of napping.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To feel how difficult it is to get his idea across.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`作者先反问 "do you really need experts to tell you that? Just look at the world around you at 2.30 in the afternoon"，意在说明午睡的重要性显而易见、无需专家论证，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What do we learn about the quantity and quality of the speaker's work?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They decline due to pointless meetings.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They depend on his ability to concentrate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They enable him to enjoy a creative career.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They are affected by the overuse of social media.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "the quality and quantity of work I can do emerges directly from my ability to concentrate"，工作质与量直接取决于专注能力，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`,stem:`What does the speaker say he finds odd?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Some bosses associate napping with laziness.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many office workers nap during work hours.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Some bosses can concentrate without napping.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many of his friends daydream in the office.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "I genuinely find it odd… their bosses insist that they take the second option and that napping is associated with laziness"，作者觉得老板们坚持不午睡、把午睡和懒惰挂钩这件事很奇怪，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section, you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C) and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`accompany`,B:`approved`,C:`bond`,D:`classified`,E:`clearing`,F:`come`,G:`daily`,H:`definitely`,I:`distractions`,J:`exclusively`,K:`inactive`,L:`occurrences`,M:`reaping`,N:`symptoms`,O:`typical`},passages:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-1`,text:`Whether you're just having a down day or a down period, taking a walk can instantly lift your mood, especially when you go outdoors. Not only can walking make you less depressed, but according to a new study, depression sufferers who took a [26] walk showed just as much improvement in their [27] as people taking medicine.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-2`,text:`In fact, 60-70 percent of the participants in the study could no longer even be [28] as depressed. Bone density may not be one of the most exciting health benefits of walking, but it's an important one. People with stronger bones avoid osteoporosis (骨质疏松症) and all the problems that [29] with it, like breaks and other disabilities. And the best way to get strong, healthy bones is by doing weight-bearing exercises like running, dancing and walking, according to a large study. But as regards bones, it's [30] use it or lose it: To keep your bones strong you have to keep exercising.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,text:`The researchers found that adults who walked regularly had better bone density throughout their lives than their [31] friends. Taking a walk can be great for [32] your head or blowing off some steam. It also provides a great opportunity to [33] with friends and family, far away from electronics and other [34] at home. Even better, you set a powerful example because when they see you [35] the benefits of walking, they'll be encouraged to walk more, too.`}]},t,{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-1`,text:`Team-building exercises have become popular for managers trying to increase organisational and team harmony and productivity. Unfortunately many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance. A paper published this week by University of Sydney researchers in Social Networks has reported participants' feelings about team-building interventions (干预), revealing ethical implications in forcing employees to take part.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-2`,text:`"Many people see team-building activities as a waste of time, so we decided to look in more depth at what's behind this," said the paper's lead researcher, Dr. Peter Matous. "Teams are formed, combined and restructured. Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned. All this is done with the aim of improving workplace efficiency, collaboration and cohesion. But does any of this work?" said Dr. Matous.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-3`,text:`The study found that team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships between team members were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive, although the researchers say some degree of openness and vulnerability is often necessary to make deep, effective connections with colleagues.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-4`,text:`"Some participants were against team-building exercises because they were implicitly compulsory. They didn't welcome management's interest in their lives beyond their direct work performance," said Matous. "Many people don't want to be forced into having fun or making friends, especially not on top of their busy jobs. They feel management is being too nosy or trying to control their lives too much."`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-5`,text:`In this study the researchers recommended a self-disclosure (表露) approach where participants were guided through a series of questions that allowed them to increasingly disclose personal information and values. The method is well-tested and has been shown to increase interpersonal closeness. However, to be successful it must be voluntary.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-6`,text:`The researchers said there are numerous schools of thought that propose differing psychological methods for strengthening relationships. "With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively to strengthen an entire collective, which is more than just the sum of individual relationships," said Dr. Matous.`}]},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-1`,text:`There are close to 58,000 homeless people in Los Angeles county. That's a shocking and tragic number for a region that's home to some of the richest people in the world. At last, the problem became so acute-and so visible-that Los Angeles took extraordinary action. The citizens of this county voted in November 2016 and again in March 2017 to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless. As a result, Los Angeles now has its best chance in decades to combat homelessness. This is an opportunity that surely all can agree must not be wasted.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-2`,text:`It is neither desirable nor morally acceptable nor practical for this county to tolerate the signs of deprivation more commonly associated with the slums (贫民窟) of Rio de Janiero. How did we get here?`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-3`,text:`For as long as there are homeless people, there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness. But contrary to popular belief, the homeless in Los Angeles are not mostly mentally ill or drug addicted or frightening, although a sizable minority meet some of those descriptions.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-4`,text:`Today, a greater and greater proportion of people living on the streets are there because of bad luck or a series of mistakes, or because the economy forgot them—they lost a job or were evicted (逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-5`,text:`The challenges are enormous, even if everyone is pulling in the same direction. That reality was driven home this month by a new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report showing that the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.`},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-6`,text:`In the world's richest nation, homelessness on this scale should be shameful and shocking. But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed. Increasingly, we are uncomfortable, annoyed, disgusted, scared or unaware. Compassion (同情心) is being replaced by resignation.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`…depression sufferers who took a __26__ walk showed just as much improvement…`,answer:`G`,explanation:`daily 每日的：抑郁症患者"每天"散步，其改善程度与服药者相当；a __ walk 中修饰名词 walk 需形容词，daily walk 搭配自然，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-1`,quote:`depression sufferers who took a [26] walk`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`…showed just as much improvement in their __27__ as people taking medicine.`,answer:`N`,explanation:`symptoms 症状：散步改善患者的"症状"，与服药者改善程度相当；their 后需名词，symptoms 符合医学语境，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-1`,quote:`improvement in their [27] as people taking medicine.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`…participants in the study could no longer even be __28__ as depressed.`,answer:`D`,explanation:`classified 归类：60-70% 的参与者甚至不再被"归为"抑郁症患者；be classified as 为固定搭配，被动语态需过去分词，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-2`,quote:`could no longer even be [28] as depressed.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`…avoid osteoporosis (骨质疏松症) and all the problems that __29__ with it…`,answer:`F`,explanation:`come 伴随：骨骼强健可避免骨质疏松及随之"而来"的各种问题；come with 为固定搭配，定语从句主语 problems 后需动词原形，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-2`,quote:`all the problems that [29] with it`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`…as regards bones, it's __30__ use it or lose it…`,answer:`H`,explanation:`definitely 确实、肯定：就骨骼而言，"确实"是用进废退，要保持骨骼强健就必须坚持锻炼；此处需副词修饰整个表述，exclusively（仅仅）语义不符，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-2`,quote:`it's [30] use it or lose it`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`…adults who walked regularly had better bone density than their __31__ friends.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`inactive 不活跃的：经常走路的成年人一生骨密度都优于"不常活动"的朋友，形成对比；修饰名词 friends 需形容词，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,quote:`than their [31] friends.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`Taking a walk can be great for __32__ your head or blowing off some steam.`,answer:`E`,explanation:`clearing 清理：clear one's head 意为"清醒头脑、理清思绪"，散步有助于理清思路或宣泄情绪；介词 for 后接动名词，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,quote:`great for [32] your head or blowing off some steam.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`It also provides a great opportunity to __33__ with friends and family…`,answer:`C`,explanation:`bond 建立联系：bond with friends and family 指与亲友建立感情纽带，远离电子产品；不定式 to 后接动词原形，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,quote:`opportunity to [33] with friends and family`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`…far away from electronics and other __34__ at home.`,answer:`I`,explanation:`distractions 分心的事物：远离电子产品和家里其他"让人分心的东西"；other 后接名词复数，与 electronics 并列，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,quote:`electronics and other [34] at home.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`…when they see you __35__ the benefits of walking, they'll be encouraged to walk more, too.`,answer:`M`,explanation:`reaping 收获：reap the benefits of walking 意为"收获散步的好处"，看到你获益，他人也会受到鼓励多走路；see sb doing 结构用现在分词，选 M。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-cp-3`,quote:`see you [35] the benefits of walking`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`The lion dancers perform from door to door to bring New Year wishes to business people.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "the dancers travel across Chinatown going from business to business to bring good luck for the coming year"——舞狮者挨家挨户给商户带来新年好运，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`The New York dance club also serves as a place for entertainment where youngsters can enjoy themselves safely.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`C`,explanation:`C 段 "the club also acts as a recreation center and safe haven (庇护所) for teenagers, with video games readily available"——俱乐部兼作青少年的娱乐中心与安全庇护所，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-C`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`Lion dancers need to have a little imagination to perform well.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 "what makes you a competent lion dancer is that there's a sense of imagination involved"——称职的舞狮者需要有想象力，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`There are a number of different versions about the origin of the lion dance.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "Stories vary about how lion dancing came to be, but most of them include a monster named Nian"——关于舞狮起源有多种说法，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Some 50% of the members of a New York lion dance club are teenagers.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`B`,explanation:`B 段 "Teenagers comprise about half of the group"——纽约舞狮团约一半成员是青少年，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-B`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`One club member says he feels proud of his cultural tradition.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`L 段 "I value my culture and tradition, being Chinese or Asian-American. I have a lot of pride in that"——俱乐部成员为自己文化传统感到自豪，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-L`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Two dancers coordinate their movements, one manipulating the lion's head and the other its body.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`G`,explanation:`G 段 "The lion head and tail are operated by two people. As one person controls the head, a second follows under a train of fabric representing the body"——一人控狮头、一人演绎狮身，两人协调动作，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-G`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`One lion dancer compares their New York dance club to a family.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "We're a big family," he said——Alvin Chau 把纽约舞狮俱乐部比作一个大家庭，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Lion dance should be handed down to future generations as part of Chinese culture.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`I 段 "passing the dance from one generation to the next was vital… this is a part of our culture"——舞狮作为中华文化的一部分应代代相传，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-I`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`One lion dancer learned how to perform from his elders.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`L 段 "I was born into it… my uncle and father were club members… Growing up, I would always watch my family and see the lion dance"——Justin Le 从长辈（叔叔、父亲）的熏陶中学会舞狮，选 L（与第41题同段，信息匹配允许一段多用）。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-mp-L`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`Why are many employees opposed to team-building exercises?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They consider such exercises annoying.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They deem these exercises counter-productive.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They see such exercises as harmful to harmony.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They find these exercises too demanding.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第一段 "many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance"——员工把团建视为"讨厌的事"（nuisance），即觉得其恼人，annoying 与 nuisance 同义，选 A；B) 适得其反、C) 有害和谐均非原文怨言所在。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-1`,quote:`many employees resent compulsory bonding and often regard these exercises as a nuisance.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What did Dr. Matous and his team do to find out whether team-building activities would improve productivity?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They relocated team leaders and their offices.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They rearranged the staff and office spaces.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They redesigned the staff's work schedules.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They reintroduced some cohesive activities.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第二段引语 "Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned… But does any of this work?"——他们重新安排员工与办公空间，以检验这些举措是否提升效率与协作，选 B；A) 只调"团队领导"与原文 staff 不符。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-2`,quote:`Staff are relocated and office spaces redesigned.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What did the study by Matous' team find about some team-building exercises?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They were intended to share personal attitudes and relationships.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They implicitly added to the vulnerability of team members.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They invariably strengthened connections among colleagues.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They were regarded as an intrusion into employees' private lives.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第三段 "team-building exercises which focused on the sharing of and intervening into personal attitudes and relationships… were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive"——这类触及私人态度与关系的团建被视为干涉、侵入，第四段 "They feel management is being too nosy" 进一步印证，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-3`,quote:`were considered too heavy-handed and intrusive`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`How can the self-disclosure approach succeed in increasing interpersonal closeness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) By allowing participants freedom to express themselves.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) By applying it to employees who volunteer to participate.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) By arranging in proper order the questions participants face.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) By guiding employees through a series of steps in team building.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第五段 "However, to be successful it must be voluntary"——自我表露法要想成功必须出于自愿，即应用于自愿参加者，选 B；C) 只是方法步骤，并非成功的前提。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-5`,quote:`However, to be successful it must be voluntary.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What does Matous think of the various psychological methods borrowed from other fields for strengthening relationships?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They must be used in combination for an entire collective.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They prioritise some psychological aspects over others.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They place too much stress on individual relationships.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have to be applied cautiously to be effective.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`末段 "With caution, many relational methods… can be borrowed from other fields. The question is how to apply them effectively"—借用的方法须谨慎、讲求有效应用，选 D；A) 把"如何有效应用于整个集体"偷换为"必须组合使用"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p1-6`,quote:`With caution, many relational methods to improve teams and organisations can be borrowed from other fields.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`Why did the citizens of Los Angeles vote to raise their own taxes?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To combat the county's homelessness.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To reform the county's service system.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To fund the development of local infrastructure.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To narrow the gap between the rich and the poor.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第一段 "voted… to raise their own taxes. The purpose of such action is to fund… housing and social services for the homeless"——增税是为无家可归者提供住房与社会服务，即应对流浪问题，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-1`,quote:`to fund an enormous multibillion-dollar, 10-year program of housing and social services for the homeless.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`What do people tend to believe about the homeless?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They seldom meet the descriptions of homelessness.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are the victims of fast economic development.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are responsible for their own condition.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They account for the majority of drug addicts.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第三段 "there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition—to see their failure to thrive as an issue of character, of moral weakness, of laziness"——人们倾向于把流浪归因于自身品格与懒惰，即认为其应为处境负责，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-3`,quote:`there is a tendency to blame the victims themselves for their condition`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What is one of the causes for more and more people in Los Angeles to become homeless?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They find it increasingly difficult to afford a place to live.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The divorce rate in the county has been on a steady rise.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They have been compelled to take low-paying jobs.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The society fails to forgive them for their mistakes.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第四段 "they lost a job or were evicted (逐出) or fled an abusive marriage just as the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving"——住房市场越来越不宽容，失业或被逐者难以负担住房，选 A；B) 离婚率上升属无根据推断。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-4`,quote:`the housing market was growing increasingly unforgiving.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What do we learn from the new Los Angeles Homeless Services Authority report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Supplying enough job opportunities for the homeless remains a challenge.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Everyone is pulling in the same direction to solve the homelessness problem.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The increase in new housing falls short of the demand of the growing homeless population.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Los Angeles' homeless condition is deteriorating faster than many people predict.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第五段报告显示 "the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing"——无家可归者增长快于新住房供应，即新房增量跟不上需求，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-5`,quote:`the county's homeless population is growing faster than the supply of new housing.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`How do most Los Angeles residents now feel about homelessness?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They are increasingly ashamed about its scale.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They find it no more scaring than it appears.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They are less and less indifferent to it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They no longer find it shocking.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`末段 "But most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed"——多数居民不再震惊也不再羞愧，选 D；A) 与原文相反，C) 原文说同情心正被听天由命取代，并非"越来越不冷漠"。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2023-06-s3-p2-6`,quote:`most Los Angeles residents are no longer either shocked or shamed.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`The Chinese government has been vigorously promoting compulsory education to ensure that every child has the opportunity to receive education. Since the Compulsory Education Law came into effect in 1986, through unremitting efforts, the goal of implementing compulsory education nationwide has been achieved. Nowadays, in China, children start primary school at the age of six and receive nine years of compulsory education from primary school to junior high school. Since the fall semester of 2008, students in the compulsory education stage have been exempt from tuition fees. With the implementation of a series of educational reform measures, the quality of compulsory education in China has also significantly improved.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2023-06-s3-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`中国政府一直大力推行义务教育(compulsory education)，以使每个儿童都享有受教育的机会。自1986年《义务教育法》生效以来,经过不懈努力,实现了在全国推行义务教育的目标。如今,在中国,儿童年满六周岁开始上小学,从小学到初中一共接受九年义务教育。从2008年秋季学期开始,义务教育阶段学生无须缴纳学费。随着一系列教育改革举措的实施,中国义务教育的质量也有显著提高。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"中国政府一直大力推行义务教育"用现在完成进行时 has been vigorously promoting compulsory education，"使每个儿童都享有受教育的机会"用 to ensure that every child has the opportunity to receive education 表目的。②"自1986年《义务教育法》生效以来"译作 Since the Compulsory Education Law came into effect in 1986；"经过不懈努力，实现了…目标"处理为 through unremitting efforts, the goal of implementing compulsory education nationwide has been achieved。③"儿童年满六周岁开始上小学，从小学到初中一共接受九年义务教育"译作 children start primary school at the age of six and receive nine years of compulsory education from primary school to junior high school。④"无须缴纳学费"用 have been exempt from tuition fees；"随着一系列教育改革举措的实施"用 With the implementation of a series of educational reform measures；末句"质量也有显著提高"用 the quality… has also significantly improved。全文以完成时与现在时为主，注意中文短句的合并与逻辑衔接。`,score:15}]}]}};export{n as default};